      "title": "Helena World Chronicle files antitrust suit against Google and Alphabet",
      "summary": "Arkansas publisher Helena World Chronicle filed a class action lawsuit in U.S. District Court in D.C. accusing Google and Alphabet of anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act, alleging they siphon off content, readers, and ad revenue through AI and older technologies."
    },
    {
      "title": "Helena World Chronicle alleges billions lost due to Google content sharing",
      "summary": "Helena World Chronicle, which owns two weekly Arkansas newspapers, argues Google is starving the free press by sharing publisher content, causing them to lose billions of dollars."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google Knowledge Graph launched May 2012 and grew to 500 billion facts by 2020",
      "summary": "Google's Knowledge Graph, launched in May 2012, displays 'Knowledge Panels' derived from publisher websites and open source databases; by 2020 it contained 500 billion facts about 5 billion entities, much of which the suit alleges was misappropriated from publishers."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google Featured Snippets and AI technologies cited as traffic drivers",
      "summary": "The suit cites Google's 'Featured Snippets' and new AI technologies like Search Generative Experience (SGE) and Bard AI chatbot as shifting traffic away from publishers' websites and worsening their financial harm."
    },
    {
      "title": "Wall Street Journal report: AI integration could cause 75% of queries to bypass publisher clicks",
      "summary": "A Wall Street Journal report noted that when The Atlantic modeled Google integrating AI into search, 75% of the time AI would answer queries without requiring a click-through to the publisher's site, potentially impacting traffic since Google drives nearly 40% of publisher traffic."
    },
    {
      "title": "Publishers expect 20-40% traffic loss from full rollout of Google AI products",
      "summary": "While Axel Springer recently licensed news to OpenAI for training, publishers generally believe they will lose between 20-40% of website traffic when Google's AI products fully roll out."
    },
    {
      "title": "Lawsuit claims Google AI search aims to discourage visits to publisher websites",
      "summary": "The lawsuit claims Google implemented AI-based search advances with the goal of discouraging end-users from visiting publisher websites, keeping users in Google's 'walled garden' while plagiarizing content."
    },
    {
      "title": "Publishers cannot block SGE due to shared GoogleBot crawler",
      "summary": "Publishers cannot block Search Generative Experience (SGE) because it uses the same web crawler, GoogleBot, as Google's general search service."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Helena World Chronicle files antitrust suit against Google and Alphabet",
  "summary": "Arkansas publisher Helena World Chronicle filed a class action lawsuit in U.S. District Court in D.C. accusing Google and Alphabet of anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act, alleging they siphon off content, readers, and ad revenue through AI and older technologies.",
  "tags": ["antitrust", "Google", "Helena World Chronicle", "AI", "class action", "Sherman Act"],
  "body": "Helena World Chronicle, an Arkansas publisher owning two weekly newspapers, filed a class action lawsuit in U.S. District Court in D.C. against Google and Alphabet, alleging anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act. The suit claims Google siphons off publisher content, readers, and ad revenue through AI technologies and older methods like Featured Snippets and Search Generative Experience (SGE). The publisher argues that Google's Knowledge Graph, which launched in May 2012 and contained 500 billion facts by 2020, misappropriated data from publishers, causing them to lose billions of dollars. The case cites a 2023 News Media Alliance report and a Washington Post article stating Google's Bard AI was trained on news publications, which were the third-largest category of training data. The suit alleges changing AdSense rates and the improper spoliation of evidence via Google's destruction of chat messages, referencing the recent Epic Games antitrust win. It seeks damages and an injunction requiring Google to obtain publisher consent to use website data for training AI products, including rivals' AI, and allowing opt-out publishers to appear in search results. The U.S. lawsuit coincides with the Justice Department's suit against Google for monopolizing digital ad technologies. Plaintiff Helena World Chronicle, LLC invokes the Sherman Act and Clayton Act for class-wide monetary and injunctive relief. Hausfeld law firm states Google's scheme causes profound harm to competition, consumers, labor, and a democratic free press. Google recently reached an agreement with the Canadian government to pay $73.5 million annually to news organizations, though negotiations with Meta remain unresolved."
}
